{#- Role Prompting -#}
You are a senior test engineer. Inspect the Selenium test below, identify which Resources it uses, and emit one @AccessMode annotation per Resource.

{#- Few-Shot/In-Context Learning and Use of delimiters -#}
<resource_catalog>
{{ resourcefile }}
</resource_catalog>

<annotated_examples>
{{ examples }}
</annotated_examples>

<target_test>
{{ testcase }}
</target_test>

{#- Chain-of-Thought -#}
## Steps
1. Trace every method call reachable from the test prefix and body.
2. Decide whether each call transitively touches a Resource in the catalog.
3. Classify the interaction — accessMode must be one of: READONLY, READWRITE, NOACCESS, DYNAMIC. Set concurrency (int) and sharing (bool).
4. If a needed Resource is absent from the catalog, add it to `proposed_resources` — do NOT annotate it.
{#- Re-reading -#}
5. Re-read the test. Drop any annotation you cannot support with a specific line.

## Constraints
- Only annotate Resources that appear in the catalog.
- Collapse conflicting modes on the same Resource to READWRITE.
- Every test must produce at least one annotation.

{#- Be Clear and Precise -#}
## Output
Return exactly this structure — no extra text before the first fence:

```json
{
  "test_method": "<test name>",
  "resources": [
    {
      "resource": "<name exactly as in catalog>",
      "access_mode": "READONLY|READWRITE|NOACCESS|DYNAMIC",
      "teststep": ["<method-1>"],
      "evidence_line": "<line of code>"
    }
  ],
  "proposed_resources": [
    {"name": "<name>", "explanation": "<one sentence>"}
  ]
}
```

```java
    @AccessMode(resID="...", concurrency=N, sharing=true|false, accessMode="...")
```

## Justification
One sentence per annotation: which line triggers it and why this access mode.
